<!--ita {"slug":"49-a-calm-guide-for-parents","title":"A calm guide for parents","category":"safety-and-judgment","excerpt":"How to talk with your child about AI apps, agree on a few family rules, and know when to ask the school or a professional for help.","reviewed":"2026-11-16","gated":false,"level":"beginner","audiences":["parents-and-families","beginners"],"check":["Many AI apps set a minimum age in their terms of use, and the age differs between apps.","Chatbots are trained to write helpful, polite replies, so they tend to sound warm and agreeable even though they are not people.","Some apps are built around characters that chat like a friend, and memory features can add details from earlier chats to later ones.","Many apps include a way to report a harmful or upsetting reply.","Chatbots can give wrong answers in a confident tone, including on schoolwork."]} -->
<p>Your child may already meet AI in a homework app, a phone keyboard, a game or a chatbot a friend showed them. You don't need to be an expert to help. Start by being curious about what they do with it. A few clear family rules can come after.</p>

<h2>Start with curiosity</h2>
<p>"What did you do at school today?" often gets the answer "Nothing." Questions about AI work better when they're specific and come without a lecture attached. You could ask, "What did you use it for this week?" or "Can you show me how it works?"</p>
<p>Another good question is "Has it ever told you something that turned out to be wrong?" Asking them to show you lets them be the expert, and showing is easier than describing.</p>
<p>Listen to the whole answer before you say anything about rules. If something surprises you, ask one more question first, such as "What happened next?" A calm reply shows your child that this is a topic they can bring to you.</p>
<p>Your child may also want to know what a chatbot is. In simple words, it writes its answer by predicting the next piece of text, again and again. It can sound sure and still be wrong. It isn't a person, even when it's friendly.</p>
[ita_why title="Why a chatbot can feel like a friend"]
<p>Chatbots are trained to sound helpful and polite, which can make them feel warm. Some apps are built around characters that chat like a friend. Memory features can add details from earlier chats to later ones, so the chatbot seems to remember your child.</p>
<p>None of this means it understands or cares. If your child talks about a chatbot as a friend, stay calm and curious. Ask what they like about it, and talk together about how it's different from friends who know them.</p>
[/ita_why]

<h2>A few family rules</h2>
<p>A few rules are usually enough. Write them with your child. Good topics are what you use AI for and what you never type in. Add what to do when an answer seems wrong or something feels upsetting.</p>
<p>For what never goes in, start with full names, the school's name and photos of people. The full list is in "What not to paste into an AI tool". For schoolwork, the school's rules come first, and they can vary from teacher to teacher. For anything upsetting, the rule is: stop and tell a grown-up. Make clear your child won't be in trouble for telling.</p>
<p>Many apps set a minimum age in their terms of use, and the age differs between apps. Check it before your child signs up, along with the settings for memory and chat history.</p>
<p>Once you've talked, a chatbot can turn your family's answers into a short agreement your child can read. Write the answers together first, then put them into the brackets of this [ita_term define="The message or instructions you type to an AI app to tell it what you want."]prompt[/ita_term], with no names:</p>
[ita_prompt title="Our family agreement"]
We are a family making simple rules for using AI apps at home. Here are our answers to four questions.
What we use AI for: [homework help and drawing ideas]
What we never type in: [our names, address, school or photos of people]
What we do when an answer seems wrong: [check a book or ask a grown-up]
What we do if something feels upsetting or strange: [stop and tell a parent]
Write these as a family agreement of five lines at most, in words a [nine]-year-old can read.
[/ita_prompt]

<h2>When to ask for help</h2>
<p>Some questions are for the school. Ask which AI apps it uses, what happens to students' data, and what is allowed for homework. Teachers can tell you the rules for each class.</p>
<p>Talk to the school counselor, your family doctor or another qualified professional if your child seems worried or withdrawn for days after something they saw. Do the same if a chat involved something frightening, sexual or unsafe. If your child may be in immediate danger, call your local emergency number. An adult or stranger may have asked your child for photos, to keep secrets or to meet. If so, contact the police, or a child-protection hotline where one exists.</p>
<p>Many apps include a way to report a harmful or upsetting reply. Use it, and keep a screenshot if you may need to show someone later. If the content is sexual and involves a child, don't save, copy or forward it. Report it to the police or the platform and follow their instructions.</p>

[ita_quiz]
[ita_q q="Your child says a chatbot is their friend. Which reply helps you understand what's going on?" answers="Explain that it's only a program, and leave it there|Ask what they like about chatting with it|Set a daily time limit for the app straight away" correct="2" why="An open question shows you what the chatbot means to your child before you decide anything."]
[ita_q q="Your child wants to use a chatbot for a school project. What should you check about the project?" answers="What the teacher allows for this project|Whether the chatbot's answers are accurate enough to copy|Whether the app keeps a history of the chats" correct="1" why="Schools and teachers set their own rules for AI in schoolwork."]
[ita_q q="Your child saw something upsetting in a chat and still seems worried days later. What is a sensible next step?" answers="Talk with them, and contact the school counselor or a doctor if the worry continues|Talk about it once, then let it go so it doesn't grow|Report the reply in the app and leave it there" correct="1" why="Talking shows them they can come to you, and a counselor or doctor can help if the worry stays."]
[/ita_quiz]

<h2>Try it</h2>
<p>Choose a calm moment for a short conversation, like a car ride or a meal, and keep screens out of it.</p>
<p>Ask one question from the first section, and listen to the whole answer before you mention any rules. Then ask your child to tell you about one thing they've used AI for, or one thing they'd like to try.</p>
<p>Before the conversation ends, agree on one family rule together, and write it somewhere you'll both see it, for example on the fridge.</p>
